@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — two generalizations formed from the existing holding
#
# Neither needed a work to be opened. Both were formed from claims that have
# stood in the graph for some time and that nobody had set beside one another,
# which is the cheapest kind of growth this project has and the kind SPEC §13
# names as the answer to a tracker that outgrows the graph.
#
# The counter-search required by SPEC §16 was carried over the compiled holding
# before either was written, and in the first case it changed the statement:
# the holding carries rankings whose ground is metaphysical and which the first
# formulation would have contradicted. The scope clause is what came out of
# that, and the cases that forced it are named at the node.
#

pmc:ProcedureJudgedByItsOwnWorking a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The procedure judged by its own working"@en, "Das Verfahren, nach seinem eigenen Arbeiten beurteilt"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission runs more than one procedure of the same kind and states a comparative judgement of how well they work, the ground it gives for the judgement is a property of the procedure — that its outcome lies in the operator's own hand, that it is repeatedly wrong, that one route is easier than another — and not a property of the persons who work it. This holds of judgements about working; it does not hold of rankings by dignity or by metaphysical order, which the same holding carries and which are grounded otherwise."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard, pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders, pmc:TheOrderOfExitsForADisease ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three come from transmissions between which no route has been proposed. The Zande material holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it and only he may work it, and records that of the poison oracle a manipulation is never even suggested; the ground is that one instrument's outcome is under one party's control and the other's is not. The Andean report says of the fire-readers, and of no other class of specialist in its list, that they were the least esteemed because they always lied; the ground is a record of being wrong. The Lakota material ranks the routes by which a disease is driven out — sweat best and easiest, then breath, then defecation, then urine, with vomiting very hard though some diseases come out no other way — and adds a rule matching the kind of healer to the kind of cause; the ground is the working of the route."@en ;
    skos:note "Why this is worth stating at all. The projects that gather this material commonly assume that a transmission explains a failure by something about the person: impurity, wrong descent, a broken taboo, insufficient resolve. That assumption is well supported and this holding is full of instances of it. What these three show is that the same transmissions are capable of the other move as well, and that they make it precisely where they have two procedures to set against each other. A comparison needs a common measure, and the measure they reach for is the procedure and not the man."@en ;
    skos:note "What the counter-search found, and it is the reason for the scope clause. The holding carries at least two graded orders whose ground is not the working of anything: the ācāra ladder, which sets the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava, the Śaiva, the right hand, the left hand and the Siddhānta to the Kaula, and the fivefold ranking of the primary devotional relations. Both are rankings, both stand inside their transmissions, and neither gives a reason that any outcome could bear on. Without the scope clause they would refute the statement on the first reading; with it they are simply a different kind of judgement, and the difference between the two kinds is itself worth having."@en ;
    skos:note "What would refute it as it stands: a transmission that compares two of its own procedures for how well they work and grounds the comparison in the persons who work them — that this instrument is less reliable because the men who handle it are of bad descent, or impure, or less well initiated. The holding contains judgements of exactly that form about individuals, and they are worth keeping apart from this. The Yoruba material has a king declare an elder a cheat and a liar and bar him from official consultations, and the Zande material has a witch-doctor called a cheat because his medicines are poor and a liar because he has no witchcraft in him. Both locate the fault in a man; neither is a comparison of procedures, and so neither settles the statement either way."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the derivation. Three claims, each a single passage, and in two of the three the comparison is reported by an outside recorder rather than stated in the transmission's own words for its own purposes. That the ground is a property of the procedure is clear in all three; that the transmissions would put it that way if asked is not established, and this node does not claim it."@en .

pmc:UnsoughtCapacityCannotBeLaidDown a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The capacity that came unsought cannot be laid down"@en, "Das ungesucht gekommene Vermögen lässt sich nicht ablegen"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission holds that a capacity or an office comes to a person without being sought, it also holds that the person cannot be rid of it at will, and it locates the impossibility in the capacity or in what conferred it rather than in a rule the person could choose to break."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:CautionAgainstLayingDownTheOffice, pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The Tungus material states that once a person has become a shaman he must go on to death or to psychic invalidity, and gives three grounds: the spirits left without a master would harm the clan and will not easily submit to a new one, the condition that compelled him to take the office may return, and the clan holds that he must not give up. The Sámi material reports a man who surrendered his drum on complaint and confessed in tears that although he had parted with it and made no other he would have the same visions as before, and that he did not know how to use his eyes since distant things were presented to them. In the first, two of the three grounds lie outside the person and one is a rule; in the second there is no rule at all, only the capacity continuing after its instrument is gone."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the inverse of nearly everything else in the holding, and that is why it is worth a node. The ordinary shape of a caution here is a warning against doing the thing wrongly or against doing it at all; these two warn against stopping. A transmission that holds its own central attainment to be irreversible has said something that bears on what the attainment is supposed to be, and it has said it against its own interest."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search over the holding found no case of an unsought capacity being laid down, and it found one thing that looks like a third witness and is not. The Inuit narrative reports second sight as a standing property of a person and expressly not as the outcome of an exercise, which satisfies the antecedent; it says nothing whatever about whether it could be given up, which is a silence and not a case. The acquired powers of the Indian material are ranked as obstacles to the goal of the practice, which is a different claim: an obstacle is something to be passed, not something that cannot be shed."@en ;
    skos:note "The weakness of the derivation is the pairing. Both transmissions are circumpolar, both were written down by outsiders, and no route between them has been examined in this holding. Two witnesses from one region do not make a statement about transmissions in general, and the way to widen it is to ask the same question of the corpora that report an office entered through an illness which is resolved only by accepting it — the Korean material is registered here and carries no node."@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry a later examination should not lose. The Tungus report gives reasons a person inside the transmission could state; the Sámi report gives a first-person complaint that the recorder heard and that includes, in the same breath, a claim of a verified hit about the recorder's own journey. The second is therefore both the better and the worse witness — better because it is the man himself, worse because the passage has an evident interest in being remarkable."@en .
